metal2vulkan: on PanVK, a texture over padded rows that a pass renders is an image over the bytes, not a shadow (Scion)

Upstream:  metal2vulkan, https://github.com/steelbrain/metal2vulkan, at 720ef1ce9ae2a2068999a8675717418b9416283e, after
           AdrenoMetal's 16 patches to it and Scion's 0001 to 0009; tools/metal/mk-metal2vulkan.sh applies them in that order,
           then this.
Licence:   GPL-2.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later, metal2vulkan's (its LICENSE); this patch is a change to it and under the same
           terms. Nothing of it is in Scion's MIT or APSL files.
Author:    the HakiOS authors (Scion), 14 November 2026.

Why. The window server's framebuffers are IOSurfaces 1125 pixels wide at rows of 4608 (and 4544) bytes. The bridge asks for
padded rows to be aliased even where a pass writes them (PaddedReadsPolicy::AliasWrites), but the runtime takes that back
where a tile store at the right or bottom edge could reach past the buffer: it assumes every driver stores whole 16x4-texel
blocks there, and 2313 rows rounded up to 2316 end past the surface's last page. So every framebuffer was a shadow image:
copied in from the surface before every command buffer and back after it, 1.2 to 1.3 ms of the GPU each way at every frame,
in two more job chains (docs/metal.md, phase 7).

What. The block a tile store writes is a fact of the driver (Device::tile_store_block). PanVK's Mali writes back each tile's
pixels inside the framebuffer descriptor's width and height and none past them, which Mesa's own linear layout relies on (it
pads no linear image's height and rounds rows only to 64 bytes): 1x1 there, the 16x4 block on every other driver.
`metal_tri --surface --size 1125 --row-bytes 4608` checks on the phone that the bytes between the rows and after the last
row keep what was there.

